// change(user);

// console.log(user.name);
// ```

// Output:

// ```text
// Rahul
// ```

// Why?

// The first operation mutates the original object.

// The second reassigns only the local parameter.

// A useful precise interview statement:

// > JavaScript passes arguments by value. For an object argument, that value is a reference to an object. Mutating through that reference affects the original object, but reassigning the parameter doesn't reassign the caller's variable.

// ---

// # 42. Is JavaScript pass-by-reference?

// The safest interview answer is:

// > JavaScript is pass-by-value. For objects, the value being passed is a reference to the object, so mutations are visible through other references to the same object.

// Avoid simply saying:

// > “Objects are passed by reference.”

// That is common shorthand, but technically imprecise.

